==========
psion/opl #2276, from hzk, 2029 chars, Sep 23 16:37 95
Comment to 2273.
----------
You can start an application easily -
1.      ROM application:

        pgname$="rom::WORD.APP"+CHR$(0)
        cmdl$="OWord"+CHR$(0)+".TXT $"+CHR$(0)+edfile$+CHR$(0)
        r%=CALL($0187,UADD(ADDR(pgname$),1),addr(cmdl$),0,0,addr(pid%))
        CALL($0688,pid%)

The above will open Word as a text editor, loading edfile$ (includes full 
path).

2.      Translated OPL program:

        pgname$="rom::SYS$PRGO"+CHR$(0)
        cmdl$="ORunOpl"+CHR$(0)+CHR$(0)+oponame$+CHR$(0)
        r%=CALL($0187,UADD(ADDR(pgname$),1),addr(cmdl$),0,0,addr(pid%))
        CALL($0688,pid%)
        
Here an OPO file named oponame$ (incl full path) will be launched.

You can check whether the lauched process is still running:

        status%=CALL($8c8d,pid%)
        IF status%<0      :REM editor closed
        
This assumes that the launching process is still active, i.e. pid% has 
not changed!

You can close down an APP or OPA by sending a message to it as follows:

        x$="X"
        r%=CALL($da8d,pid%,UADD(ADDR(x$),1),1)   REM send shutdown msg.
        
If closing an OPA, make sure the OPA handles close down messages 
properly, as shown on page 113 in the 3a programming manual.  i.e. the 
OPA should be waiting for an event (e.g. GETEVENT) and should interpret a 
system message "X" as a closedown request.

All this was extracted from the OPL for PocPac, my packet radio program.  
Hopefully I haven't missed anything out!  By the way, it's worth checking 
that the launched process is still active before trying to close it down. 
It's also worth checking that the process has actually been started 
before trying to close it, etc.

When launching things from OPL you run the risk of leaving them running 
after closing down the main program.  A sub process can launch another, 
and you can get into a right pickle!  So if your main program receives a 
closedown message, it should check if it has launched anything still 
active, and close it down if necessary!

Roger


